ed pedigree is  correct for the chromosome 9 QT l eb9  coming from Devon surprise then we  should be able to observe a shared hafla  type near a benign in Devon  surprise and in all of our landmark  early blight resistant inbreds now this  plot which is based on the output of the  a bababa test shows us that this is  indeed the case now  this analysis looks for the evidence of  introgression x' in triplet snip  contrast giving a D statistic that can  be used to paint chromosomes by ancestry  and also to visualize homologous hafla  types and what we find is that in all of  the early blight resistant entries they  share a solid red block of snips near  the ev9 locus and this suggests say  Devon's a shared Devon surprise  introgression in contrast snips that are  more consistent with a high in 1706  ancestry are colored in blue now another  way to find regions of high sequence  similarity is to window the genome and  then to use a clustering algorithm to  intelligently group the resulting alpha  types now here you can see an example  where I've projected 30 tomatoes from  four different clades into a two  dimensional PCA space for a single  genomic window near the eb9 KTL then I  used the k-means algorithm to cluster  the samples and found that it perfectly  separated the early blight resistant and  susceptible large rooted cultivars from  each other from two cherry tomato types  and from the wild species solanum fabric  IDs now this type of unsupervised  clustering analysis is really nice  because it's computationally pretty  trivial and because we can improve our  ability to distinguish among observable  haplotypes as we add additional  sequences to our database now once a  sample in a window is classified as a  member of a group we can then ask if  that sample is in the same or different  group from some putative ancestral line  of interest we can then color the areas  that have the same k-means clustering  nation to visualize homologous  haplotypes and even introgression now  here is one such plot of chromosome 9  inclusive of the 8 resistant samples  that I talked about early  they're blocked by this green resistant  bar at the top and then two random  susceptible samples from the pedigree I  described earlier at the bottom and the  genomic windows were colored if in any  window they were in the same cluster as  Devin surprise and the resistant  tomatoes are also sorted according to  their hypothesized ancestry with Devin  surprise the putative donor followed by  C 1943 and CBR to NCC gob r1 and then  our three most recent Cornell releases  and what's really cool about a plot like  this is you can actually watch the  haplotype from Devin's surprise decay  over time with phenotypic selection  going from a large introgression in  Campbell's 1943 to a relatively small  one into North Carolina tomatoes and  here on the right we've zoomed in to a  95% confidence interval for the eb9  locus the black ticks denote the  confidence interval boundaries and the  most predictive spin marker at the  center and we can clearly see that the  smallest contiguous Devin surprise  haplotype was present in the sample and  ccel be our 1a and this gives us a new  resistance haplotype size that's about  33% smaller than before or about 1.2  megabase pairs and furthermore these  data strongly suggest that our modern  early blight stem resistance traces back  directly to an early 20th century tomato  developed in Devon England which was  then improved by dr. Renard and through  significant public sector breeding  efforts spanning more than 40 years now  I don't really have time today to tell  you the whole story for the other  resistance Llosa here but I did want to  show you that we can do use the same  methods to trace our early blight 5  resistance locust to an unimproved  tamanna breeding lying named Hawaii 7 9  9 8 and in this case we were actually  able to shrink our interval size by more  than 60% over our prior boundaries so  this demonstrate