on and Devon  surprise and and make our best claim  that that's the ultimate source in this  resistance let's see like the historic  approach thank you very much do you  think that on reducing the haplotype  sizes for finer mapping you could be  confounding the effects of other  potential causal qtls  do I think that reducing the haplotype  sizes for finer mapping I  I'm sure I totally understand the  question there but I don't think that  where we are confounding the effects I  think we could be leaving out other QTL  and in fact I have already left out  several QTL that were of minor effect  here we really wanted to focus on the  largest QTL of interest large defect QTL  of interest because that's really what  the breeding programs are most  interested in when they're trying to  bring these into their background and  then another question what tool were  using for haplotype visualization so I  used Python for creating the to do the  k-means clustering analysis and then the  these plots are actually based on  they're actually just ggplot bar plots  in which I've stripped out all of the  unnecessary background detail all the  grids and everything and used facet wrap  to stack all of the entries on top of  each other so that we can do comparisons  across entries nice I think that is one  more question do we have markers for key  tl5 and key tl9 that seed companies  could use yes I mean we currently have  markers that we are using in our own  breeding program but really the purpose  for that are the motivating factor for  that all of this work is to define  markers that will be useful for all  backgrounds so yes we currently have  markers that could be used they may not  be polymorphic in all backgrounds but we  hope that by the end of this work they  will be robust enough for any and all  programs to use them okay I think there  are no more questions let's thank Taylor  for your nice work and move on to the  third speaker thank you  this has been a production of Corn